⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186512932 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=88,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.44.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=88(0.44)=38.72>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=88(1-0.44)=49.28>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=88$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,\\frac{0.44(1-0.44)}{88}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,0.0028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.52).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.52) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.52-0.44}{\\sqrt{0.0028}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.51) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.51) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186512932 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=88,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.44.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=88(0.44)=38.72>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=88(1-0.44)=49.28>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=88$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.44,\\frac{0.44(1-0.44)}{88}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,0.0028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.52).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.52) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{0.52-0.44}{\\sqrt{0.0028}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.51) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.51) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186512937 1074 "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th brick, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a pallet. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(3,{0.25}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 500,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(40,{4}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected pallet with bricks. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{500}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =500\\cdot 3+40 \\\\  & =1\\,540, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =500\\cdot {0.25}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =47.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(1\\,540,47.25).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W<1\\,545),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<1\\,545) & =P(Z<\\frac{1\\,545-1\\,540}{\\sqrt{47.25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.73) \\\\  & =Φ(0.73)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.73)≈0.7673.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<1\\,545) & ≈Φ(0.73) \\\\  & ≈0.7673.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186512937 1074 "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th brick, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a pallet. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(3,{0.25}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 500,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(40,{4}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected pallet with bricks. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{500}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =500\\cdot 3+40 \\\\  & =1\\,540, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =500\\cdot {0.25}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =47.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(1\\,540,47.25).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W<1\\,545),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<1\\,545) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{1\\,545-1\\,540}{\\sqrt{47.25}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.73) \\\\  & =Φ(0.73)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.73)≈0.7673.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<1\\,545) & ≈Φ(0.73) \\\\  & ≈0.7673.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186512941 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \