igned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186519559 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-3y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(-3))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\left({4}^{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (16-2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186519563 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x, & \\,y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}).$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186519563 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x, & \\,y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P\\left(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}\\right).$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186519563 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the co