}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+1].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int [{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1]\\text{d}x & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+1]-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]-\\frac{1}{2}x+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186525986 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+1\\right].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left[{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1\\right]\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+1\\right]-1\\right]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}-1\\right]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\right]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\right]-\\frac{1}{2}x+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\en