316205b537c9a457b81b9fb3ba88e63602bd0aaebc90f7cedfef05863cd.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3))+2.$\n\n![](images/b3/b3e358c3c3d2371b5c0af932857bf34065a079d223a0d1fdfcbd09cab32a8506.png)" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186526043 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4}f\\left(\\frac{1}{3}x-1\\right)+2=\\frac{1}{4}f\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)\\right)+2\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{4}f\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)\\right)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=3$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\frac{1}{3}x\\right).$\n\n![](images/b8/b85eed09d973abb632eb92a3a9f2abb7a2c9aa09061778d9df22eac5d59ee86e.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)\\right).$\n\n![](images/d0/d03aa20f465906c8cbcc65a1f42b208d7f0071732d47598158fc1c81ee6222ef.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)\\right).$\n\n![](images/d2/d2ccc316205b537c9a457b81b9fb3ba88e63602bd0aaebc90f7cedfef05863cd.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)\\right)+2.$\n\n![](images/b3/b3e358c3c3d2371b5c0af932857bf34065a079d223a0d1fdfcbd09cab32a8506.png)" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186526048 1074 "The area of the region bound