{1}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+\\frac{4}{(x+6)}+\\frac{1}{2(x+6)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186534018 1074 "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+6}+\\frac{B}{(x+6)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+6)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+6)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+11=A(x+6)^{2}+B(x+6)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6)^{2}+8(-6)+11 & =A(-6+6)^{2}+B(-6+6)+C \\\\ -1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+8(0)+11 & =A(0+6)^{2}+B(0+6)+C \\\\ 11 & =36A+6B-1 \\\\ 36A+6B & =12 \\\\ 6A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-5$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5)^{2}+8(-5)+11 & =A(-5+6)^{2}+B(-5+6)+C \\\\ -4 & =A+B-1 \\\\ A+B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the equations $6A+B=2$ and $A+B=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5A & =2-(-3) \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-3 \\\\ B & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+6}-\\frac{4}{(x+6)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+6)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+11}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{x+6}-\\frac{4}{(x+6)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+6)^{3}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{(x+6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+6)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+\\frac{4}{(x+6)}+\\frac{1}{2(x+6)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186534021 1071 "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]?$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186534021 1071 "Which of the following gives the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0\\right]?$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186534021 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\nTherefore, the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [175921865340