\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n}\\cdot n}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{9+5n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\cdot \\frac{5n}{n}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{9}{n}+\\frac{5n}{n}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[n]{n}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{n}+5\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1}{0+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186534981 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]?$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186534981 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ for $t\\in \\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi \\right]?$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186534981 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{e}^