ges/8a/8a17502c300fae56e9f2e1706d23a3e5a7d3233eebff9a77958d117a0135adb0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=6\\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the resulting solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(6\\sqrt{y}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{y}+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24\\sqrt{y}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24{y}^{1/2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(36\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+24\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}+4y)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(18(1)^{2}+16(1)^{3/2}+4(1))-0] \\\\  & =38\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186534988 1074 "Notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left function and which is the right, let's draw our horizontal arrow.\n\n![](images/8a/8a17502c300fae56e9f2e1706d23a3e5a7d3233eebff9a77958d117a0135adb0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=6\\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the resulting solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(6\\sqrt{y}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{y}+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24\\sqrt{y}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24{y}^{1/2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\left(36\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+24\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}+4y\\right)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(18(1)^{2}+16(1)^{3/2}+4(1))-0] \\\\  & =38\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186534990 1071 "![](images/cb/cbff0c974c790ec4287be1dcff8aa62d0da0aa5e77fa5ec577633f5f2cad6a5d.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186534990 1071 "![](images/cb/cbff0c974c790ec4287be1dcff8aa62d0da0aa5e77fa5ec577633f5f2cad6a5d.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186534996 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186534996 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\left(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186535001 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/2a/2a2e4be26d18e408bf6e4580c72ec2f3c08eed1750d38e0716f06a32d3c03e7c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=(x-1)^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,1-(x-1)^{4}\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-2(x-1)^{4}+(x-1)^{8})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(x-\\frac{2}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{9}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi ([2-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(1)^{9}\\text{ }]-[0-\\frac{2}{5}(-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(-1)^{9}\\text{ }]) \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi }{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186535001 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/2a/2a2e4be26d18e408bf6e4580c72ec2f3c08eed1750d38e0716f06a32d3c03e7c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=(x-1)^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,1-(x-1)^{4}\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-2(x-1)^{4}+(x-1)^{8})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\left(x-\\frac{2}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{9}\\text{ }\\right)|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\left(\\left[2-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(1)^{9}\\text{ }\\right]-\\left[0-\\frac{2}{5}(-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(-1)^{9}\\text{ }\\right]\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi }{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186535002 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/ad/ad089d39428498123469a2ac7895f7d04b8b3ff9dac18a1698462bfc7fabae99.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/97/975f68d06bb52e162ad792987899509f4090c03ec1b55af6335982a2951f6bbf.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4-(x-1)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,5-(4-(x-1)^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(1+(x-1)^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+2(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}((x-1)^{4}+2(x-1)^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)^{3}+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}+2-(-\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}+0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{43}{15}+\\frac{13}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186535002 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/ad/ad089d39428498123469a2ac7895f7d04b8b3ff9dac18a1698462bfc7fabae99.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/97/975f68d06bb52e162ad792987899509f4090c03ec1b55af6335982a2951f6bbf.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4-(x-1)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,5-(4-(x-1)^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(1+(x-1)^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+2(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}((x-1)^{4}+2(x-1)^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)^{3}+x\\right)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}+2-\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}+0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi \\left(\\frac{43}{15}+\\frac{13}{15}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186535006 1074 "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/73/73fc1e2cad0969a629e2c2c4a55ce8ac66c16b67fe2984e8b75dc6aa3a5eeace.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt{4-x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,(\\sqrt{4-x})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}((4-x)-2\\sqrt{4-x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(5-x-2\\sqrt{4-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (5{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{4-x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi (5x{|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{4}{3}(4-x)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi ((15-0)-(\\frac{9}{2}-0)+\\frac{4}{3}(1-8)) \\\\  & =\\pi (15-\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{28}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186535006 1074 "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/73/73fc1e2cad0969a629e2c2c4a55ce8ac66c16b67fe2984e8b75dc6aa3a5eeace.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt{4-x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,(\\sqrt{4-x})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}((4-x)-2\\sqrt{4-x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(5-x-2\\sqrt{4-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (5{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{4-x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi \\left(5x{|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{4}{3}(4-x)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi \\left((15-0)-\\left(\\frac{9}{2}-0\\right)+\\frac{4}{3}(1-8)\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi \\left(15-\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{28}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186535008 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\,⟹\\,x=1-\\frac{1}{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=-2$ to $y=-1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/23/239113c4d2bbca26c694796f6f17bd26bebf47697dad53f7af832088213fd4c9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1-\\frac{1}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{[\\,(2)-(1-\\frac{1}{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{(1+\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186535008 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\,⟹\\,x=1-\\frac{1}{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=-2$ to $y=-1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/23/239113c4d2bbca26c694796f6f17bd26bebf47697dad53f7af832088213fd4c9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1-\\frac{1}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\left[\\,(2)-\\left(1-\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\,\\right]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{y}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\left(1+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186535012 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 false] [17592186535012 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186535016 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/37/37665aa7ea5388a1162ca68a1662aaa86d6369f083d7563dbf27c521c13a7c72.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\