\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12(2{x}^{2}-y)=0 \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4x)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=2{x}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-4x) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{4}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of the last equation are $x=0,1.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({0}^{3})-12(0)(0)+{0}^{3}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our first point is $(0,0,1).$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({1}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({1}^{3})-12(1)(2)+{2}^{3}+1=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, our second point is $(1,2,-7).$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7).$" 13194139545757 17592186576411 true] [17592186538936 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-12y \\\\  & =12(2{x}^{2}-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =-12x+3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3({y}^{2}-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨12(2{x}^{2}-y),\\,3({y}^{2}-4x),\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\